---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Contact from '../components/Contact.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
---
<Page
  title="About Hausla"
  eyebrow="About"
  lede="Hausla is a free companion for families facing cancer in India. It is built slowly and carefully, by one person, with the goal of making the cancer journey a little less confusing and a little less lonely."
  updated="2026-11-11"
  toc={[
    { id: 'why', label: 'Why Hausla exists' },
    { id: 'who-for', label: 'Who it is for' },
    { id: 'what-we-do', label: 'What you will find here' },
    { id: 'what-we-dont', label: "What Hausla doesn't do" },
    { id: 'who-builds', label: 'Who builds it' },
    { id: 'free', label: 'Free, always' },
    { id: 'contact', label: 'Get in touch' },
  ]}
>
  <h2 id="why">Why Hausla exists</h2>
  <p>
    A cancer diagnosis arrives with a flood of new words, new places and new decisions, usually at the moment a
    family is least able to take them in. Information exists, but it is scattered across hospital websites,
    government portals, research papers and forums. Much of it is written for doctors, much of it is written for
    other countries, and very little of it answers the questions families actually ask in the first weeks:
    <em>Where do we go? What will it cost? Who can help us pay? What happens next? Are we doing this right?</em>
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Hausla</strong> (हौसला) means courage: the spirit that keeps you going when things are hard. The name
    isn't a promise that things will turn out well. It is a recognition of how much it takes to keep showing up,
    for yourself or for someone you love.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-for">Who it is for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Caregivers and families</strong>, especially those travelling to a big city for treatment. They are our first focus, because they often carry the research, the paperwork and the hospital queues.</li>
    <li><strong>People who have just been diagnosed</strong>, and people in or after treatment.</li>
    <li><strong>Students and researchers</strong> who want to understand cancer science and cancer care in India.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>We are starting with Delhi and the surrounding region, and in English, with Hindi next.</p>

  <h2 id="what-we-do">What you will find here</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Guides</strong> that explain cancer, treatment and everyday practicalities in plain language, summarised from official and medical sources.</li>
    <li><strong>A directory of care</strong>: hospitals, helplines and financial-assistance schemes, each with the date we last checked it.</li>
    <li><strong>Support</strong>: mental-health helplines, carefully chosen communities, and stories from people who have been through it, always linked to the person who wrote them.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Much of this is still being written. We publish each part only when it is ready.</p>

  <h2 id="what-we-dont">What Hausla doesn't do</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Hausla is <strong>not a medical service</strong>. We cannot diagnose, recommend treatments or tell you what
      will happen. Your doctors know your situation; we try to help you understand it and ask better questions.
      Please read our <a href={url('/disclaimer')}>disclaimer</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="who-builds">Who builds it</h2>
  <p>
    Hausla is an independent, personal project. Its founder has chosen to remain anonymous, so that the focus stays
    on the people the site serves rather than on the person behind it. Because you can't judge us by a name, we ask
    you to judge us by how we work: every page shows its sources and when it was last checked, and our
    <a href={url('/editorial-policy')}>editorial policy</a> explains exactly how content is made, including that
    drafts are prepared with the help of AI tools and checked against sources by a person before publishing.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not yet have a medical professional reviewing our content. Until we do, medical pages are clearly marked
    as summaries of official sources, not reviewed advice. If you are a doctor, nurse, pharmacist or medical student
    and would like to help review, we would be very grateful to hear from you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="free">Free, always</h2>
  <p>
    Hausla will always be free to use. There are no accounts, no advertisements and no tracking, and no hospital or
    company can pay to be listed or featured. See our <a href={url('/privacy')}>privacy policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact">Get in touch</h2>
  <p>To suggest a correction, offer help or share feedback, write to <Contact />.</p>
</Page>
